description:
Apologies in advance for the AI-generated description, but it did the debugging and I think it did a good job of writing this up.

## Summary
On Windows with endpoint security software running, Posit Assistant turns fail because the atomic save of `index.json` (write `index.json.tmp`, then `rename` over `index.json`) gets a transient `EPERM`. There is no retry, so the turn is aborted. Once it starts, every subsequent message hits it again, including in new conversations and after Reload Window, which makes the Assistant unusable.

Users see two notifications:

1. On the failing turn:
   > Failed to project conversation 93abac12-2608-4aaa-98b3-5d8b3c0f83c1 into index.json
2. On the next attempt:
   > The turn finished, but its terminal state could not be saved. Repair is required before another turn.

The automatic repair (on the next turn, and in `reconcileStartup` after a reload) does run and logs `matched-terminal-repaired`. But it goes through the same rename, so the next turn fails again.

## Environment
- Posit Assistant: **1.7.0 and 1.6.1** (I rolled back to 1.6.1 and got the same failure)
- Positron: 2026.09.1 build 2 (Code 1.130.0)
- OS: Windows 11 Enterprise 10.0.26200, x64
- Endpoint security: Trellix Endpoint Security (on-access scanning) and CrowdStrike Falcon, both managed by IT. Workspace is on a local NTFS drive, not a network share.
- Model provider: GitHub Copilot via vscode.lm

## Log (`~/.posit/assistant/logs/extension-host.log`)
```
[warn]  Failed to save index file Error: EPERM: operation not permitted, rename 'C:\Users\<user>\.posit\assistant\workspaces\f525823e034ba7681a9b5ea9\index.json.tmp' -> 'C:\Users\<user>\.posit\assistant\workspaces\f525823e034ba7681a9b5ea9\index.json'
[error] Turn execution failed Error: Failed to project conversation 40049157-6314-482a-b078-764388a69e48 into index.json
[error] Conversation execution error Error: Failed to project conversation 40049157-6314-482a-b078-764388a69e48 into index.json
[warn]  Failed to save index file Error: EPERM: operation not permitted, rename '...\index.json.tmp' -> '...\index.json'
...
[info]  Reconciled running marker for 40049157-…: turn 9f312ea0-… -> matched-terminal-repaired
```
On 2026-10-08 this happened on every message I sent, in both 1.6.1 and 1.7.0. `execution-state.json` was left with entries under `running`, and `index.json.tmp` was left on disk.

## Reproduction of the underlying Windows behavior
This script runs the same `writeFile(tmp)` → `rename(tmp, dest)` pattern under Positron's own Node (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 Positron.exe test.js`), in `~/.posit/assistant/workspaces/`:

```js
const fs = require("fs"), path = require("path"), os = require("os");
const d = path.join(os.homedir(), ".posit", "assistant", "workspaces", "_rename_test");
fs.mkdirSync(d, { recursive: true });
const f = path.join(d, "index.json"); fs.writeFileSync(f, "{}");
(async () => {
  let fails = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
    await fs.promises.writeFile(f + ".tmp", JSON.stringify({ i, pad: "x".repeat(4000) }));
    try { await fs.promises.rename(f + ".tmp", f); } catch (e) { fails++; }
  }
  console.log(`fails=${fails}/200`);
})();
```

Result: **165/200 renames fail with `EPERM`**. With a 50 ms gap between iterations there are 0 failures, which fits an on-access scanner briefly holding a handle on the newly written file.

## Fix I verified locally
The Positron filesystem wrapper (`class UY`, `fileSystem = new UY`, in `dist/extension.js`) does a bare rename:

```js
async rename(e,r){await sE.promises.rename(e,r)}
```

I replaced it locally with a bounded retry:

```js
async rename(e,r){
  for (let a = 0;; a++) {
    try { await sE.promises.rename(e,r); return; }
    catch (x) {
      if (a >= 20 || !["EPERM","EACCES","EBUSY"].includes(x?.code)) throw x;
      await new Promise(z => setTimeout(z, Math.min(25 * (a + 1), 100)));
    }
  }
}
```

With this change, the same 200-iteration test gives **0/200 failures** (average rename 34 ms, max 103 ms). In real use, the stuck conversations repaired on reload and new turns complete normally.

The bundle already has an equivalent helper used for the process-mutex code (`ixn` / `kL`, which retries `EPERM`/`EACCES`/`EBUSY` with backoff for up to 2 s). It isn't used by the conversation store's `jC` / `pK` atomic-write helpers, which go through `UY.rename`.

## Suggestions
- Retry transient `EPERM`/`EACCES`/`EBUSY` in `UY.rename`, or in the atomic-write helpers. This is the same approach as `graceful-fs` and VS Code's own Windows rename handling.
- Consider making a failed `index.json` projection non-fatal. By that point `conversation.json` has already been written, and the index can be re-projected later. That way a transient index failure wouldn't block further turns.

## Workarounds
- An antivirus exclusion for `%USERPROFILE%\.posit\assistant\` would likely prevent this, but many users on managed machines can't set that themselves.
- Locally patching `UY.rename` as above works, but any extension update undoes it.

Found while investigating item 7 of #16380.

## What happens

If a user doesn't open Positron for longer than `interpreters.discoveryCache.maxAgeDays` (default 30), every launch after that runs a full interpreter discovery, as if the cache were empty. Startup stays slow until the user runs **Interpreter: Clear Interpreter Cache**, which they have no reason to know about.

## Why

Each cache entry stores the date it was first added (`firstSeen`), and expiry is based on that date. When an entry is past the limit:

1. The cache hides it (`_freshEntries()` in `runtimeDiscoveryCache.ts`), so the language looks empty and the next launch runs a full discovery as a cold start (`managersNeedingFullDiscovery()` in `runtimeStartup.ts`).
2. The cleanup before discovery (`discoverAllRuntimes()` in `runtimeStartup.ts`) finds entries to delete with `getEntries()`, which returns only entries that haven't expired. The expired entry stays.
3. Discovery finds the same interpreter again. `upsert()` sees an entry already exists for that path and keeps its old `firstSeen`.
4. The entry is still expired, so the next launch runs a full discovery again. This repeats on every launch.

The setting's description says a cached interpreter is reused for that many days "before it is rediscovered", and the comment above `RUNTIME_DISCOVERY_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS_SETTING` says an entry "must be re-validated by a real discovery pass at least once every 30 days." So the limit is meant to force a fresh discovery every 30 days, not to expire the entry for good.

## Repro

This is hard to reproduce by hand, so there are two Vitest tests below. Both fail on `main` and pass once the bug is fixed.

**`runtimeDiscoveryCache.vitest.ts`** ("makes an expired entry fresh again when discovery re-finds it"): writes an entry, moves the clock past 30 days, and writes the same path again. `getEntries` returns `[]` instead of the entry.

**`runtimeStartup.vitest.ts`** ("goes back to warm starts after a full discovery re-finds an expired interpreter"): opens Positron four times with the real cache, reloaded from storage each time. Each launch records whether it skipped discovery (warm start) or ran it (cold start).

| Launch | Time | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Day 0 | cold start (empty cache) | cold start |
| 2 | Day 0, 1 hour later | warm start | warm start |
| 3 | Day 31 | cold start (entries expired) | cold start |
| 4 | Day 31, 1 hour later | warm start | **cold start** |

Launch 2 shows the test can produce a warm start, so the cold start at launch 4 is the bug. The test calls the real `managersNeedingFullDiscovery()`. It doesn't run the discovery itself, because that needs the whole startup sequence. Instead it copies the three things discovery does to the cache: record the time of the pass, delete the entries that haven't expired, and write each found interpreter back.

